course: everyday
lesson: 508
title: Fighting, ignoring each other and sharing a room
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In Wakefield, Brandon and Kyle, 9 and 10, fight every single day: kicks under the table, «you're a div» (a div is an idiot), «you're adopted» (he isn't, and being adopted wouldn't be an insult anyway). Their dad drives a lorry and is often away, and one night Mum puts her head in her hands: «I'm done, I'm proper done with you two.» Brothers and sisters close in age often fight a lot, and it usually gets better. But fighting that hurts someone every day is not okay, and a grown-up has to step in.
explain: Sharing a room means rows and midnight laughs. In Hull, Wren snores, Tilda throws a pillow, Wren throws a slipper, and by midnight it's full war until Dad shouts up the stairs, «Pack it in, the pair of yer!» Five minutes later they are giggling under the covers. In Liverpool, twins Kieron and Kaden have bunk beds; every night Kaden kicks the top bunk from below. «Pack it in, ya divvy!» The next night Kieron pops a balloon over Kaden's head, and both crack up. Pranks are fine while both are laughing.
explain: Rooms need rules. In Sydney, Shanaya and Priya-Lee stick a tape line down the middle of their bedroom: «Cross it and you're cactus» (Aussie for done for, finished). Then Priya-Lee needs to get out, and the door is on Shanaya's side. They argue, then laugh at how daft it is, and leave a gap by the door. In Ohio, Cooper and Hayes fight over the bathroom every morning, so Mom buys a kitchen timer: «Four minutes each, then you're out, no cap.» They grumble, but the fights stop. A fair rule is boring, and it works.
explain: Some fights are about privacy. In Belfast, Cara, 11, reads her sister Orla's messages on the shared iPad. Orla screams, «That's PRIVATE!» Even in families that share everything, people need things that are only theirs. In East London, Ibby and Yusuf fight over the PlayStation until Yusuf throws a controller. Dad takes the console away for a whole week. «Bare long,» moans Ibby (London slang: a massive pain). Fighting over a thing often means losing the thing.
explain: And sometimes siblings drift. In Llanelli, Gethin, 9, and Owain, 12, used to be best mates; now Owain is on his phone all the time, and they walk to school without a word. It hurts, and it often comes back later; one film night they both still like can help. In Rotorua, Rawiri and his sister Mere ignore each other completely at school, then build forts at home for hours. A mate asks, «Is Mere even your sister?» «Nah yeah, she is, eh» (in New Zealand and Australia «nah yeah» means yes). Both ways are real.
fact: Brothers and sisters close in age often fight a lot, and it usually gets better as they grow.
fact: Fighting that hurts someone every day is not okay; a grown-up has to step in.
fact: When a row heats up, walking away is a strong move, not a weak one.
fact: Sharing a room brings rows about noise, mess and lights, and also midnight laughs.
fact: Room rules made together, like lights-out time and whose shelf is whose, help a lot.
fact: A boundary in a shared room has to be fair to both, so both can still reach the door.
fact: Simple rules like a timer for the bathroom stop many fights; a fair rule is boring but it works.
fact: Privacy matters even in families; reading someone else's messages without asking is a betrayal.
fact: Pranks are fine only while both people are laughing.
fact: Fighting over a thing, like a console, often means losing it.
fact: Siblings often drift apart when one becomes a teenager, and they can grow close again later.
fact: Many siblings act differently at school and at home; both are real.
fact: «Div» and «divvy» mean idiot; «pack it in» means stop it; «cactus» is Aussie for done for; «bare long» is London slang for a big pain; «nah yeah» means yes.
exercise: What did Mum say after Brandon and Kyle fought again? | «I'm done, I'm proper done with you two» | «Keep going, it's good for you two» | «Just make sure Dad doesn't hear it» | «Whoever wins gets extra pudding»
exercise: When must a grown-up step in to sibling fights? | When someone gets hurt every day | Never, kids should always sort it alone | Only when the noise wakes the neighbours | Only when a window or a plate gets broken
exercise: What started the pillow war between Tilda and Wren? | Wren's snoring | A fight about the telly remote | Tilda's new pyjamas | A missing hairbrush
exercise: What did Dad shout up the stairs? | «Pack it in, the pair of yer!» | «Carry on, I'm watching the footy!» | «Who threw the first slipper, then?» | «Right, both of you out in the garden»
exercise: What happened five minutes after the pillow war? | They giggled under the covers | They stopped speaking for a week | Wren moved into the spare room | Tilda went to sleep on the sofa
exercise: What does «cactus» mean in Australian slang? | Done for | Very spiky | Thirsty | Lucky
exercise: What was the problem with Shanaya and Priya-Lee's tape line? | The door was on Shanaya's side | The tape kept falling off the carpet | Mum told them tape wasn't allowed | The line went right through the bed
exercise: How did Cooper and Hayes's mom stop the bathroom fights? | A timer, four minutes each | She built a second bathroom | She let the oldest go first every morning | She made them both get up an hour earlier
exercise: Why was Orla so upset with Cara? | Cara read her private messages | Cara broke the shared iPad | Cara used up all the battery | Cara deleted her favourite game
exercise: Who sleeps on the bottom bunk, Kieron or Kaden? | Kaden | Kieron | They take turns every night | Neither, they share the top
exercise: Why was the balloon prank okay? | Both of them were laughing | Kaden deserved a scare for the kicking | Pranks are always fine between twins | Their mum said pranks were allowed
exercise: Why did Dad take the PlayStation away? | Yusuf threw a controller in a fight | The boys forgot to do their homework | It was broken and needed mending | Dad wanted to play it himself
exercise: What does «bare long» mean in East London? | A big pain | Very tall | Too far | Long time no see
exercise: Why do Gethin and Owain walk to school in silence now? | Owain is 12 and always on his phone | They had a fight about a bike | Gethin lost his voice | Owain moved schools
exercise: What might help Gethin and Owain feel close again? | A film night they both still like | Gethin hiding Owain's phone for a week | Telling Mum Owain is mean every day | Never speaking to him again
exercise: «Is Mere even your sister?» «Nah yeah, she is, eh». What does Rawiri mean? | Yes | No | Not sure | Not any more
test: Kyle: «you're adopted, div, nobody wanted ya». Is «adopted» an insult? | No, it isn't, and he isn't anyway | Yes, being adopted is something to hide | Yes, adopted kids aren't real family | No, but only because he said div first
test: Bathroom timer: 4 minutes each, one after the other. Cooper goes first at 7:10, then Hayes, then little sister Jo. When must Jo be out? | 7:22 | 7:14 | 7:18 | 7:26
test: «I'm well raging, she keeps crossing my line, I'm gonna rip all her posters». What's a better move? | Agree new rules together that are fair to both | Rip the posters before she can do yours | Draw a second line closer to her bed | Lock her out of the room all evening
test: Gethin: «Owain doesn't even like me now, he's always on his phone». What's most likely true? | Teenagers often drift, and it can come back | Owain hates Gethin forever now | Phones make brothers stop loving you | Gethin must have done something awful
test: Cara: «it's a shared iPad, so I can read whatever's on it, innit». Right? | No, messages are still private | Yes, shared means nothing is private | Yes, if she's the older one | No, but only on weekdays
test: What would most likely happen if Ibby and Yusuf kept fighting over the next console too? | They'd lose that one as well | Dad would buy them one each | They'd never lose it again | Dad would join in the fight
test: Kaden kicks Kieron's bunk; Kieron is crying because it really hurts his back. Is it still a fine prank? | No, a prank stops being fine when one isn't laughing | Yes, twins can do anything to each other | Yes, pranks are always just a laugh | No, because pranks are always banned
test: «Rawiri ignores his sister at school, he must hate her lol». What's truer? | Siblings often act different at school and home | He secretly can't stand her at home too | They must have had a big fight that morning | Ignoring someone always means hating them
test: In a sitcom, two sisters share a room and fight in every scene, nothing else. What's real life like? | Rows and also midnight laughs | Fighting all day with no laughs at all | Never any fights, just fun | Only boys fight over rooms
test: Tilda and Wren: lights out is 9:00, and the giggling went on until 9:45. Wren says it was «barely ten minutes». How long was it really? | 45 minutes | 10 minutes | 15 minutes | 9 minutes
test: Mum: «Brandon, your brother's winding you up again, what d'you do?» Which is the strong move? | Walk away before it heats up | Kick him first to show who's boss | Shout louder than him | Break something of his
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises frequent quarrelling between siblings of similar age? | It is common and generally diminishes with time, though daily injury is unacceptable | It is invariably a sign of permanent hatred between them | It never occurs in families that love one another | It should be resolved by physical combat
exam: Identify the arrangement most conducive to the cessation of disputes concerning the use of a shared bathroom. | A fixed time allocation measured by a timer | The admission of the strongest sibling first | The prohibition of bathroom use before school | The unrestricted use of the room by all
exam: Ascertain the most appropriate view of the perusal by one sibling of another's private messages upon a shared device. | It breaches privacy even within a family | It is permissible because the device is shared | It is permissible for the elder sibling only | It is required to ensure the younger's safety
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a boundary drawn across a shared bedroom. | It must be fair to both, so both can reach the door | It ought to favour whichever sibling drew it first | It is to be enforced irrespective of access to the door | It renders all further discussion of the room unnecessary
